{"url": "https://systematictraders.substack.com/p/caveats-of-simple-factor-timing-strategies", "title": "Caveats of Simple Factor Timing Strategies", "engine": "exa", "published": "2025-05-20", "snippet": "The study considers the standard Fama-French factors for the U.S. stock market: size (SMB), value (HML), profitability (RMW), investment (CMA), and momentum (WML). The sample period runs from July 1963 until December 2024, the longest period for which data is available for all five factors.\n...\nis excluded since the focus is specifically on timing factors rather than the market. The market factor is fundamentally different from other factors as it represents a long position in the entire equity market and a short position in the risk-free asset. In contrast, the other factors go long stocks with certain characteristics and short those with\n...\nShort-term factor momentum exploits the tendency of factor returns to be directionally similar from one month to the next. Applying this strategy to the five Fama-French factors by taking positions of 40%, 30%, 20%, 10%, and 0% based on the ranking of their returns over the previous month generates an outperformance of 2.8% per annum with a highly significant t-statistic of 4.9 over the 1963-2024 sample period.\n...\nHowever, this strategy faces several important caveats. First is performance decay - from 2011 onward (post-GFC period), the annualized outperformance dwindles to a mere 0.4% per annum. Second is the high turnover involved with rotating in and out of different factors every month, which significantly erodes performance after accounting for transaction costs. Finally, short-term strategies can be sensitive to the choice of reb", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 17198, "excerpt": "Written by David Blitz, Robeco Quantitative Investments Introduction and Data Various simple factor timing strategies appear remarkably effective in historical backtests. However, they may be prone to performance decay over time, questionable profitability after transaction costs, or implicit exposures to previously established phenomena. This analysis is not motivated by fundamental skepticism to", "jev": {"concrete": 0.95, "evidence": "backtest", "evidence_confidence": 1.0, "market": "us_stocks_etfs", "us_long_only": 0.36, "recent": 0.91, "code": 0.05}, "priority": 0.838}
{"url": "https://hannahattar.com/factor-momentum.html", "title": "Factor Momentum: Replication & Extension | Hannah Attar", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "Replicating and extending Ehsani & Linnainmaa (2022) and Arnott, Kalesnik & Linnainmaa (2023). Do equity risk factors exhibit momentum — and is stock-level momentum simply a reflection of this factor-level autocorrelation? \n\nData\n...\nReferences Ehsani & Linnainmaa (2022), Arnott et al. (2023)\n...\n- Factors are autocorrelated. HML and PMU show statistically significant persistence — a factor that performed well over the prior year tends to continue performing well.\n...\n- UMD dynamically times other factors. Conditional correlations reveal that UMD's relationship with other factors flips sign depending on recent performance — invisible in unconditional statistics.\n...\n- Factor momentum generates significant alpha. Both TSFM and CSFM produce returns unexplained by the five-factor model. CSFM is completely independent of stock-level UMD.\n...\n- Momentum concentrates in high-eigenvalue PCs. Consistent with the theoretical prediction that persistent sentiment affects only the most systematic factor combinations.\n...\n- Implementation requires scale. With only four factors, the momentum signal is too noisy for reliable long-only rotation. The academic results rely on 20–47 factors.\n...\nEach factor is constructed using the standard Fama-French 2×3 sort methodology. Every month, stocks are independently sorted into two size groups (NYSE median) and three signal groups (NYSE 30th/70th percentiles), producing six value-weighted portfolios. The factor return averages the long-short spread ", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 8914, "excerpt": "← Back to Projects Factor Momentum: Replication & Extension Replicating and extending Ehsani & Linnainmaa (2022) and Arnott, Kalesnik & Linnainmaa (2023). Do equity risk factors exhibit momentum — and is stock-level momentum simply a reflection of this factor-level autocorrelation? Project Report · Asset Pricing · Replication 0.45 CSFM Sharpe Ratio Cross-sectional factor momentum, annualized. 3.33", "jev": {"concrete": 0.96, "evidence": "backtest", "evidence_confidence": 1.0, "market": "us_stocks_etfs", "us_long_only": 0.17, "recent": 0.19, "code": 0.12}, "priority": 0.646}
{"url": "https://insights.finominal.com/research-factor-timing-via-market-momentum", "title": "Factor Timing via Market Momentum", "engine": "exa", "published": "2024-08-26", "snippet": "Factor Timing via Market Momentum\n...\n# Factor Timing via Market Momentum\n...\nAugust 2024. Reading Time: 10 Minutes. Author: Nicolas Rabener.\n...\n- Theoretically, investors allocate to risky stocks when bullish and defensive ones when bearish\n- A simple stock market momentum model does not validate this\n- Factor investing should be pursued across all market regimes\n...\nThere is little evidence that market timing works, but how about factor timing? We might be poor at predicting where the S&P 500 will be in three or twelve months, but should this not be easier for deciding on whether to bet on value or growth stocks?\n...\nTheoretically, investors should prefer risky stocks when the stock market is in a bull and defensive stocks in a bear market. Investors make style changes slowly, therefore we might be able to exploit such trends by measuring the momentum of the stock market. When the momentum is positive, buy cheap and small-cap stocks, when negative, buy high-quality and low-volatility stocks.\n...\nIn this research article, we will exploit factor timing via stock market momentum.\n...\nFirst, we evaluate the returns of five factors in the U.S. stock market by using the performance of the stock market as a signal. Long-short factor returns for the size, value, profitability, and momentum factors are sourced from the Kenneth R. French data library, while low beta factor returns are from AQR.\n...\nWe create two portfolios for each factor based on the 12-month performance of the U.S", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 2252, "excerpt": "Factor Timing via Market Momentum Does it work? August 2024. Reading Time: 10 Minutes. Author: Nicolas Rabener. SUMMARY - Theoretically, investors allocate to risky stocks when bullish and defensive ones when bearish - A simple stock market momentum model does not validate this - Factor investing should be pursued across all market regimes INTRODUCTION There is little evidence that market timing w", "jev": {"concrete": 0.88, "evidence": "backtest", "evidence_confidence": 0.95, "market": "us_stocks_etfs", "us_long_only": 0.43, "recent": 0.37, "code": 0.07}, "priority": 0.634}
{"url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13504851.2025.2472032", "title": "Cross-sectional factor momentum: evidence from multiple formation periods: Applied Economics Letters: Vol 33 , No 13  - Get Access", "engine": "exa", "published": "2025-02-28", "snippet": "We revisit cross-sectional factor momentum by exploring a range of formation periods. Recent- and intermediate-past horizons, jointly and severally, not only produce statistically and economically significant factor momentum, remaining robust to established factors, but also capture its distinct aspects, with neither spanning the other. Subsample analyses show that the recent-past factor momentum premium persists post-publication, suggesting it is unlikely driven by data mining, while the intermediate-past premium varies with the publication and significance of factors.\n...\n## Notes\n\n 1 See Goyal and Jegadeesh (Citation 2018) for a comparison of TS and CS momentum.\n\n 2 See Sharifkhani and Simutin (Citation 2021) for a recent explanation of this effect through industry trade networks.\n\n 3 See also, among others, Yan and Yu (Citation 2023), Fieberg, Metko, and Zaremba (Citation 2024), Käfer, Mörke, and Wiest (Citation 2024), Cakici et al. (Citation 2025) for recent discussions on factor momentum.\n\n 4 In other words, in terms of Novy-Marx’s (Citation 2012) n–m strategy, which refers to the strategy based on portfolios sorted on returns from n to m months prior to their formation, we consider 1–1, 2–6, 7–12, and 13–60 strategies.\n...\n5 For example, Jensen, Kelly, and Pedersen (Citation 2023) cite Lakonishok, Shleifer, and Vishny (Citation 1994) for the free cash flow-to-price factor. It is split into 530 Pre-Cite (November 1950–December 1994) and 348 Post-Cite (January 1995–Decem", "extra": {}, "read_via": "exa_fetch", "text_chars": 5012, "excerpt": "# Cross-sectional factor momentum: evidence from multiple formation periods: Applied Economics Letters: Vol 33 , No 13 - Get Access URL: https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13504851.2025.2472032 Published: 2025-02-28 Author: Donghyun Kim Cross-sectional factor momentum: evidence from multiple formation periods: Applied Economics Letters: Vol 33 , No 13 - Get Access # Cross-sectional facto", "jev": {"concrete": 0.48, "evidence": "backtest", "evidence_confidence": 0.6, "market": "other", "us_long_only": 0.39, "recent": 0.75, "code": 0.07}, "priority": 0.23}
{"url": "https://www.aimspress.com/article/doi/10.3934/DSFE.2024023?viewType=HTML", "title": "A comprehensive high pure momentum equity timing ...", "engine": "exa", "published": "2024-11-29", "snippet": "A comprehensive high pure momentum equity timing framework using the Kalman filter and ARIMA forecasting\n...\n# A comprehensive high pure momentum equity timing framework using the Kalman filter and ARIMA forecasting\n...\n- Received: 28 June 2024 Revised: 30 October 2024 Accepted: 27 November 2024 Published: 29 November 2024 \n- JEL Codes: G11, G12, G17, C22\n...\n- The pursuit of higher returns has led to a growing interest in factor timing as a strategy to enhance portfolio returns. Momentum is a popular factor, which involves buying securities that have shown consistent price appreciation over the past 3 to 12 months or past few years, with the expectation that the trend will continue and reducing exposure to those that consistently declined. An important part of a factor timing strategy is in the portfolio optimization process. This article aimed to first construct a large capitalization pure momentum portfolio, which included a dynamic stringent portfolio construction process criteria for selecting stocks estimated from historical data. Second, as a part of the portfolio's risk management strategy, the Kalman filter was applied to the historical performance of this portfolio. Lastly, the ARIMA forecast was used to estimate expected performance and the confidence intervals. The empirical results showed that this pure equity momentum factor timing framework with the Kalman filter together with the ARIMA (autoregressive integrated moving average) forecasting methodology was iter", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 10610, "excerpt": "Research article - The pursuit of higher returns has led to a growing interest in factor timing as a strategy to enhance portfolio returns. Momentum is a popular factor, which involves buying securities that have shown consistent price appreciation over the past 3 to 12 months or past few years, with the expectation that the trend will continue and reducing exposure to those that consistently decl", "jev": {"concrete": 0.46, "evidence": "backtest", "evidence_confidence": 0.26, "market": "other", "us_long_only": 0.54, "recent": 0.41, "code": 0.06}, "priority": 0.228}
{"url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/0015198X.2025.2474385", "title": "Optimal Factor Timing in a High-Dimensional Setting", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "We develop a framework for equity factor timing in a high-dimensional setting when the number of factors and factor return predictors can be large. To ensure good out-of-sample performance, the approach is disciplined by shrinkage that effectively expresses a degree of skepticism about outsized gains from timing. In our empirical application, the predictors include macroeconomic variables and factor-specific characteristics spreads between the long and short legs of the factors. We find sizable gains from timing equity factors, including for factors constructed only from large-cap stocks.\n...\nExisting research on factor timing has mostly focused on single predictors, or very small numbers of predictors and portfolios, with mixed success. In this paper, we show that gains from factor timing are achievable when many predictors of factor returns are used in combination within a portfolio optimization framework. In our empirical analysis, we use the four factors of Fama and French (Citation2015) and a much larger set of factors from Jensen, Kelly, and Pedersen (Citation2023) as the factors to be timed. We use macroeconomic variables as predictor variables, as well as factor-specific predictors such as the value spread, momentum, and other characteristics spreads between the long and short leg of the factors.\n...\nOur paper builds on earlier work in this area. Asness, Chandra, Ilmanen, and Israel (Citation2017) study factor timing with each factor’s value spread. They find only sma", "extra": {}, "read_via": "jina", "text_chars": 58918, "excerpt": "Title: Optimal Factor Timing in a High-Dimensional Setting URL Source: https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/0015198X.2025.2474385 Markdown Content: ## Abstract We develop a framework for equity factor timing in a high-dimensional setting when the number of factors and factor return predictors can be large. To ensure good out-of-sample performance, the approach is disciplined by shrinkage t", "jev": {"concrete": 0.44, "evidence": "backtest", "evidence_confidence": 0.43, "market": "other", "us_long_only": 0.3, "recent": 0.28, "code": 0.05}, "priority": 0.155}
{"url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0378426624002462", "title": "Factor momentum versus price momentum: Insights from ...", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "Does factor momentum drive stock price momentum? We examine this relationship across 51 countries. Factor momentum proves strong across many markets and international portfolios, independent of typical return predictability drivers. However, its ability to capture stock momentum profits depends on methodological and dataset choices. Empirical factor momentum cannot entirely subsume stock or industry momentum globally. Conversely, price momentum often better explains its factor counterpart than vice versa. Notably, factor momentum based on principal components is more robust, capturing a major share of price momentum gains in developed and emerging markets. Our findings challenge the view that momentum merely times other factors rather than constituting a distinct anomaly.\n...\nIn this study, we comprehensively revisit this relationship. Rather than examining one type of factor momentum in a single country, we take a holistic approach and explore various empirical designs in a broad global context. We scrutinize the performance of 145 anomalies across 51 markets. We consider two principal versions of factor momentum: in empirical anomalies and their principal components (PC). We implement them in multiple ways to reach a simple yet unambiguous conclusion: one cannot capture all the stock or industry momentum profits just by timing other factors.\n...\nOur results contribute in five fundamental ways. First, we extend the prior factor momentum evidence to 51 global markets. We find", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 16070, "excerpt": "Research article Factor momentum versus price momentum: Insights from international markets Author links open overlay panelNusret Cakici a , Christian Fieberg b c d , Daniel Metko e , Adam Zaremba f g h Skip to main contentSkip to article Keywords JEL classification Introduction The momentum effect is one of the most pervasive anomalies ever documented, driving prices across various asset classes:", "jev": {"concrete": 0.27, "evidence": "backtest", "evidence_confidence": 0.96, "market": "other", "us_long_only": 0.35, "recent": 0.19, "code": 0.04}, "priority": 0.097}
{"url": "https://www.aeaweb.org/conference/2024/program/paper/8GNzb57Z", "title": "Timing the Factor Zoo", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "Abstract We provide a comprehensive analysis of the timing success for equity risk factors. Our analysis covers over 300 risk factors (factor zoo) and a high dimensional set of predictors. The performance of almost all groups of factors can be improved through timing, with improvements being highest for profitability and value factors. Past factor returns and volatility stand out as the most successful individual predictors of factor returns. However, both are dominated by aggregating many predictors using partial least squares. The median improvement of a timed vs. untimed factor is about 2% p.a. A timed multifactor portfolio leads to a 8.6% increase in annualized return relative to its naively timed counterpart.\n...\nEmpirical asset pricing research has identified a staggering quantity of priced risk factors. While it may be challenging to rationalize all these factors as independent sources of sys tematic risk, it is clear that one needs a multifactor model to explain the cross-section of asset returns. In light of the empirical asset pricing literature, it is also uncontroversial that risk premia vary conditionally over time. At the market level, for example, Fama and French (1988) find that returns are predictable by the dividend-price ratio. This opens the arena for market timing, but, in a multifactor world, the more general question concerns the timing of all sources of systematic risk – factor timing. Given the plethora of factors, it is no surprise that a large numbe", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 420565, "excerpt": "%PDF-1.5 %���� 1 0 obj > endobj 6 0 obj > endobj 2 0 obj > endobj 3 0 obj > endobj 4 0 obj > endobj 5 0 obj > endobj 7 0 obj > endobj 8 0 obj > endobj 9 0 obj > endobj 10 0 obj > endobj 11 0 obj > endobj 12 0 obj > endobj 13 0 obj > endobj 14 0 obj > endobj 15 0 obj > endobj 16 0 obj > endobj 17 0 obj > endobj 18 0 obj > stream x�+�\u0002\u0000\u0000�\u0000| endstream endobj 19 0 obj > stream x��\u0018�v�Dt��Ц�Ȝz:/�X��&�\u000f", "jev": {"concrete": 0.26, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.94, "market": "other", "us_long_only": 0.42, "recent": 0.24, "code": 0.06}, "priority": 0.018}
{"url": "https://www.robeco.com/files/docm/docu-202307-a-prudent-route-to-effective-factor-timing.pdf", "title": "A prudent route to effective factor timing", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "Equity factor investing has gained significant popularity in recent years as investors seek to tap into the factor premiums associated with certain stock characteristics. The various factor portfolio returns exhibit distinct cycles, tempting investors to try their hand at dynamically adjusting their factor allocation. However, the key question remains: how can investors best time these factors to maximize returns while minimizing risk? In this article, we discuss and explore the promises and pitfalls of factor timing and provide an empirical investigation of the benefits of multi-factor diversification. Our findings show that a Dynamic 1/N multi-factor approach presents a prudent route to effective equity factor timing by combining low strategy turnover with the pervasive factor momentum effect, while at the same time maintaining a proper factor diversification.\n...\n- July 2\n...\nin practice.\n...\nIn regards to factor timing, Dichtl et al. (2019) provide evidence of the predictability of timing signals based on cross-sectional factor characteristics such as factor momentum, crowding or valuation spreads, but the authors also found some support for the use of fundamental time series predictors. Yet, they caution that such predictability is hard to capture after considering realistic transaction costs in real-life portfolio implementations. 4\n...\nIndeed, capturing the short-term trend in factor returns via a factor momentum signal promises additional alpha, although this is meani", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 821631, "excerpt": "%PDF-1.7 %���� 1 0 obj >/Metadata 750 0 R/ViewerPreferences 751 0 R>> endobj 2 0 obj > endobj 3 0 obj > endobj 4 0 obj >/ExtGState >/Font >/ProcSet[/PDF/Text/ImageB/ImageC/ImageI] >>/MediaBox[ 0 0 595.32 841.92] /Contents 5 0 R/Group >/Tabs/S/StructParents 0>> endobj 5 0 obj > stream x�ŖMO�@\u0010��� �H*e���^ !%\u0010>*!�`�\u0003��\u0006'�!1u�V���5\u0004B�m��m.ql� �2MPq��\u0006z\u0019r�^��[�З��9�,�$;[����Q ���Ȑ*�f�rA�\u0015\u0013T\\\u001a���\u0001���\u0004�", "jev": {"concrete": 0.24, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.74, "market": "other", "us_long_only": 0.45, "recent": 0.31, "code": 0.03}, "priority": 0.017}
{"url": "https://www.cfm.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/180-2020-09-Is-Factor-Momentum-More-than-Stock-Momentum.pdf", "title": "Is Factor Momentum More than Stock Momentum? ∗", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "Abstract Yes, but only at short lags. In this paper we investigate the relationship between factor momentum and stock momentum. Using a sample of 72 factors documented in the literature, we first replicate earlier findings that factor momentum exists and works both directionally and cross-sectionally. We then ask if factor momentum is spanned by stock momentum. A simple spanning test reveals that after controlling for stock momentum and factor exposure, statistically significant Sharpe ratios only belong to implementations which include the last month of returns. We conclude this study with a simple theoretical model that captures these forces: (1) there is stock-level mean reversion at short lags and momentum at longer lags, (2) there is stock and factor momentum at all lags and (3) there is natural comovement between the PNLs of stock and factor momentums at all horizons.\n...\nThis paper analyzes factor momentum. In recent years, a body of literature in asset pricing has analyzed the properties of a large number of risk factors (Harvey and Zhu (2016), McLean and Pontiff (2016), Hou and Zhang (2019), Giglio et al. (2020)). These factors are constructed by sorting stocks along various characteristics and are evidence that stock returns are predictable and co-move. As it turns out, recent research suggests that factor returns are themselves predictable (Cohen et al. (2003),Haddad et al. (2020)) and in particular exhibit momentum (Ehsani and Lin nainmaa, 2019): A factor that was", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 386951, "excerpt": "%PDF-1.5 %� 112 0 obj > stream x�}XK�۸\u0011��W�HUY\u0013\u0012| ��l���x�R�I�j� \u0012\u0012\u0011S�\u0002��(�>�\u0000��e f�h4\u001ah���n���.���& �?�����R��Ide�{9�j��j�\u0016����3���,M��/��}�����z=���